    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT5G64860-MONOMER" textLabel="disproportionating enzyme" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9LV91" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">D-enzyme is a 4-alpha-D-glucanotransferase. It transfers maltose units from one 1,4-alpha-D-glucan to another. Malto-oligosaccharides are the preferred substrates in-vitro. The role of D-enzyme was not clear before. The Arabidopsis DPE1 is the first D-enzyme which was shown to be involved in starch degradation. The knockout mutant of Dpe1 accumulated a much higher level of malto-oligosaccharides during the dark period for starch degradation. The role of DPE1 was indicated to create longer malto-oligosaccharides from shorter ones so that the longer ones can become substrates for other starch degradation enzymes such as beta-amylase or starch phosphorylase.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G64860-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT5G64860.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Cazy" value="GH77" />
